In Java, a class can only extend one parent class, also known as a superclass or base class. This is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming and is enforced by the Java language.

According to the Java Language Specification, a class can extend only one class, and this is achieved using the `extends` keyword. For example:
```java
public class MyClass extends ParentClass {
    // ...
}
```
There is no built-in mechanism in Java that allows a class to extend more than one class. If you need to create a class that inherits behavior from multiple classes, you can use interfaces, which can be implemented by a class. Alternatively, you can use composition, where a class contains instances of other classes, to achieve a similar effect.

